265
00:10:21.324 --> 00:10:24.443
he was so well-regarded Tony Blair had him as a...

266
00:10:24.443 --> 00:10:26.504
Had him along with him for a week.

267
00:10:26.504 --> 00:10:29.422
Just him in the entire day-

268
00:10:29.422 --> 00:10:30.255
Photographing?

269
00:10:30.255 --> 00:10:33.674
Yeah, photograph from the entire day.

270
00:10:33.674 --> 00:10:37.662
He only shot in black and white for a long time.

271
00:10:37.662 --> 00:10:39.245
He only use Leicas.

272
00:10:41.529 --> 00:10:44.351
He used to quote Canadian photographer, Ted Grant.

273
00:10:44.351 --> 00:10:45.402
"If you photograph in color,

274
00:10:45.402 --> 00:10:46.510
you see the color of their clothes.

275
00:10:46.510 --> 00:10:48.143
But if you photograph in black and white,

276
00:10:48.143 --> 00:10:51.073
you see the color of their soul."

277
00:10:51.073 --> 00:10:55.040
Just a great guy from Northern England who knocked

278
00:10:55.040 --> 00:10:57.298
on the door of his local paper and begged for a job.

279
00:10:57.298 --> 00:10:58.740
They made him an assistant photographer

280
00:10:58.740 --> 00:11:00.549
and basically never turned back.

281
00:11:00.549 --> 00:11:01.698
That was in the 70s.

282
00:11:01.698 --> 00:11:04.198
And I met him because in 2007,

283
00:11:06.453 --> 00:11:08.380
I did a story for men's health.

284
00:11:08.380 --> 00:11:12.081
A feature on these doctors and healthcare workers,

285
00:11:12.081 --> 00:11:14.081
both American and Korean

286
00:11:15.389 --> 00:11:18.222
in this ethnic provinces of Burma.

287
00:11:20.145 --> 00:11:20.978
But they were...

288
00:11:20.978 --> 00:11:23.629
A lot of them were expatriated into Thailand

289
00:11:23.629 --> 00:11:27.475
and their base of operations was in Mesa, Thailand.

290
00:11:27.475 --> 00:11:30.203
And Tom was the photographer that came with me

291
00:11:30.203 --> 00:11:31.585
on this feature,

292
00:11:31.585 --> 00:11:34.769
as I followed them into displaced people's camps.

293
00:11:34.769 --> 00:11:37.543
Or we did together to displace people's camps.

294
00:11:37.543 --> 00:11:40.492
And these were villagers, who've been...

295
00:11:40.492 --> 00:11:41.854
Had their villages torched

296
00:11:41.854 --> 00:11:43.514
and were runoff their ancestral homeland.

297
00:11:43.514 --> 00:11:46.256
And we're trying to flee to the safety of Thailand,

298
00:11:46.256 --> 00:11:49.406
but had to live in these penned areas on the river,

299
00:11:49.406 --> 00:11:50.525
in these camps.

300
00:11:50.525 --> 00:11:55.005
And aid workers were kind of giving them food and medicine.

301
00:11:55.005 --> 00:11:57.387
And that was the story of current state,

302
00:11:57.387 --> 00:11:59.210
much of current state for years.

303
00:11:59.210 --> 00:12:02.210
And Tom and I snuck over the border.

304
00:12:04.159 --> 00:12:05.909
We went to this camp.

305
00:12:06.849 --> 00:12:09.987
People who just had their homes burned down

306
00:12:09.987 --> 00:12:12.258
and spent a week together.

307
00:12:12.258 --> 00:12:16.290
First in Bangkok then Mesa, then Karen State.

308
00:12:16.290 --> 00:12:18.019
And at that time 2007,

309
00:12:18.019 --> 00:12:19.825
I was still kind of,

310
00:12:19.825 --> 00:12:22.271
wondering if this was gonna work as a job.

311
00:12:22.271 --> 00:12:24.674
I started in 2000 was writing grants

312
00:12:24.674 --> 00:12:26.703
and doing what I had to do.

313
00:12:26.703 --> 00:12:30.053
2004, I started getting bigger publications.

314
00:12:30.053 --> 00:12:32.064
We started LA Yoga and all of that.

315
00:12:32.064 --> 00:12:32.897
But by 2007,

316
00:12:32.897 --> 00:12:36.210
I was still very much in the Eviction Notice phase,

317
00:12:36.210 --> 00:12:37.623
just starting with Lonely Planet.

318
00:12:37.623 --> 00:12:40.235
So things are just starting to get better.

319
00:12:40.235 --> 00:12:42.485
And Tom he would remind me,

320
00:12:44.594 --> 00:12:46.192
"We are lions mate.

321
00:12:46.192 --> 00:12:47.953
We are lions."

322
00:12:47.953 --> 00:12:51.613
Just remind me that we have to remain confident.

323
00:12:51.613 --> 00:12:53.373
We may have to remain optimistic.

324
00:12:53.373 --> 00:12:56.208
We have to be able to fight through all the adversity

325
00:12:56.208 --> 00:12:59.600
that comes with trying to do this job in a climate

326
00:12:59.600 --> 00:13:01.336
that is not conducive to that.

327
00:13:01.336 --> 00:13:05.446
Because if you're getting paid months after the expenses

328
00:13:05.446 --> 00:13:06.868
come through, nobody cares.

329
00:13:06.868 --> 00:13:09.203
Your bills are still due.

330
00:13:09.203 --> 00:13:11.976
And then you have to deal with editors

331
00:13:11.976 --> 00:13:13.157
that are kind of fickle.

332
00:13:13.157 --> 00:13:13.990
They can be fickle.

333
00:13:13.990 --> 00:13:15.375
They can kill a story.

334
00:13:15.375 --> 00:13:17.636
They can mess with a story.

335
00:13:17.636 --> 00:13:20.576
Not that I have that problem with editors in general.

336
00:13:20.576 --> 00:13:21.928
But it does happen.

337
00:13:21.928 --> 00:13:26.263
And Tom used to refer to him lovingly as the desk fuckwits.

338
00:13:26.263 --> 00:13:29.768
And not that he thought that of those of the editors

339
00:13:29.768 --> 00:13:31.414
that he worked with, but just that,

340
00:13:31.414 --> 00:13:34.495
it's a way of thinking so that you can maintain

341
00:13:34.495 --> 00:13:36.557
some sort of sanity while you go and do this.

342
00:13:36.557 --> 00:13:38.415
And meanwhile you're photographing

343
00:13:38.415 --> 00:13:42.687
or interviewing people who've been through hell.

344
00:13:42.687 --> 00:13:45.807
Most recently his biggest story was the migration

345
00:13:45.807 --> 00:13:47.946
of people from Syria.

346
00:13:47.946 --> 00:13:50.686
And into, I think it was Lesbos into Greece,

347
00:13:50.686 --> 00:13:52.477
that massive influx.

348
00:13:52.477 --> 00:13:54.111
He was there for that.

349
00:13:54.111 --> 00:13:56.270
To see him shoot, he's rolling on the ground.

350
00:13:56.270 --> 00:13:59.678
He's got knee pads, he's got elbow pads.

351
00:13:59.678 --> 00:14:01.231
I mean, he's a full body experience.

352
00:14:01.231 --> 00:14:03.759
This is into his 60's, doing this.

353
00:14:03.759 --> 00:14:05.592
And then he had a show

354
00:14:07.644 --> 00:14:10.827
at the Annenberg Space for photography in Century City.

355
00:14:10.827 --> 00:14:11.731
That's the last time I saw him.

356
00:14:11.731 --> 00:14:12.823
And I didn't even know he was sick.

357
00:14:12.823 --> 00:14:14.500
He kind of kept that under wraps.

358
00:14:14.500 --> 00:14:15.542
How did he perish?

359
00:14:15.542 --> 00:14:16.970
He had cancer.

360
00:14:16.970 --> 00:14:19.182
And he just didn't tell anybody really.

361
00:14:19.182 --> 00:14:21.521
I guess it's really close, close friends knew,

362
00:14:21.521 --> 00:14:23.182
his wife knew.

363
00:14:23.182 --> 00:14:24.640
He was the type of guy.

364
00:14:24.640 --> 00:14:26.340
He married the love of his life.

365
00:14:26.340 --> 00:14:27.173
It turned out to be,

366
00:14:27.173 --> 00:14:30.337
but it was his best friend's wife and he died,

367
00:14:30.337 --> 00:14:31.170
the best friend died.

368
00:14:31.170 --> 00:14:32.893
And then he ends up marrying.

369
00:14:32.893 --> 00:14:35.310
A very Conrad Anker in a way.

370
00:14:36.440 --> 00:14:37.947
Same with Julie's dad.

371
00:14:37.947 --> 00:14:39.947
Actually is a similar thing.

372
00:14:39.947 --> 00:14:43.827
And she's now had to bury both of those guys.

373
00:14:43.827 --> 00:14:46.727
And so the memorial, I think is today,

374
00:14:46.727 --> 00:14:48.175
or it's this week.

375
00:14:48.175 --> 00:14:49.029
This is most reasonable.

376
00:14:49.029 --> 00:14:51.395
Like I said, Extraordinary Women.

377
00:14:51.395 --> 00:14:53.944
It's some amazing photographs.

378
00:14:53.944 --> 00:14:56.373
The guy's truly a legend in the business,

379
00:14:56.373 --> 00:15:00.173
and a great, great mentor to me and a great soul.

380
00:15:00.173 --> 00:15:01.766
And I miss him and I love him.

381
00:15:01.766 --> 00:15:04.862
And I just wanted to share who he was with you guys.

382
00:15:04.862 --> 00:15:05.783
Sorry for your loss.

383
00:15:05.783 --> 00:15:08.726
How long ago was that the exhibit was going on?

384
00:15:08.726 --> 00:15:10.244
That was 2016.

385
00:15:10.244 --> 00:15:11.327
Oh, so it was five years ago.

386
00:15:11.327 --> 00:15:12.366
But I spoke with him.

387
00:15:12.366 --> 00:15:14.345
We communicated in 2020,

388
00:15:14.345 --> 00:15:16.782
right before Zuma was born.

389
00:15:16.782 --> 00:15:19.813
Yeah, when this book came out.

390
00:15:19.813 --> 00:15:21.542
Life is short man.

391
00:15:21.542 --> 00:15:26.271
On that subject, I've got a Memorial to share as well.

392
00:15:26.271 --> 00:15:30.604
My friend, Jason Plummer passed away this last week.

393
00:15:33.753 --> 00:15:34.824
Too soon.

394
00:15:34.824 --> 00:15:36.755
I'm not quite sure that there's clarity

395
00:15:36.755 --> 00:15:39.184
on how he passed away.

396
00:15:39.184 --> 00:15:41.053
It was either an aneurysm or a heart attack.

397
00:15:41.053 --> 00:15:44.054
I think he was on a stationary bike at the time.

398
00:15:44.054 --> 00:15:47.816
Jason was a teammate of mine at Stanford

399
00:15:47.816 --> 00:15:50.896
an exceptionally talented swimmer.

400
00:15:50.896 --> 00:15:53.434
Commonwealth games champion.

401
00:15:53.434 --> 00:15:55.095
Distance freestyler from Brisbane.

402
00:15:55.095 --> 00:15:58.293
He was on the 88 Australian Olympic team.

403
00:15:58.293 --> 00:16:01.013
He was world surf race champion.

404
00:16:01.013 --> 00:16:02.623
This guy was very talented,

405
00:16:02.623 --> 00:16:04.203
but he was also kind of a wild man.

406
00:16:04.203 --> 00:16:05.884
He was a free spirit.

407
00:16:05.884 --> 00:16:07.801
He was the class clown.

408
00:16:08.684 --> 00:16:13.684
He was, I think it's fair to say, not keen on authority.

409
00:16:14.114 --> 00:16:16.083
He was kind of a good time Charlie.

410
00:16:16.083 --> 00:16:18.242
And was always the life of the party.

411
00:16:18.242 --> 00:16:20.875
And I had plenty of good times with him.

412
00:16:20.875 --> 00:16:25.875
He had this history of clashing with Skip Kenny, our coach,

413
00:16:26.595 --> 00:16:30.762
who was a Marine sniper and liked to run the team.

414
00:16:31.614 --> 00:16:34.114
Like it was a military outfit.

415
00:16:35.945 --> 00:16:39.195
Which Skip is a very complicated person

416
00:16:42.122 --> 00:16:45.121
and has a very complicated legacy.

417
00:16:45.121 --> 00:16:48.443
He was a guy who put team above all.

418
00:16:48.443 --> 00:16:51.504
And I learned a lot about how you build teams

419
00:16:51.504 --> 00:16:54.864
and how you create cohesion amidst...

420
00:16:54.864 --> 00:16:58.122
Amongst individuals for a greater purpose.

421
00:16:58.122 --> 00:17:01.644
But he also kind of rained on this idea of fear,

422
00:17:01.644 --> 00:17:03.574
which didn't really work for me.

423
00:17:03.574 --> 00:17:07.891
And he was a guy who, if you were a favorite of his,

424
00:17:07.891 --> 00:17:09.042
it worked really well.

425
00:17:09.042 --> 00:17:11.312
But if you were kind of on the outs or somebody

426
00:17:11.312 --> 00:17:15.584
that he didn't quite get along with, it was a bumpy road.

427
00:17:15.584 --> 00:17:18.761
And Jason was certainly one of those people.

428
00:17:18.761 --> 00:17:23.511
And there was this huge scandal in 2007 where it came out

429
00:17:24.762 --> 00:17:29.012
that Skip had expunged swimmers that he didn't like

430
00:17:29.911 --> 00:17:32.133
from the Stanford record books.

431
00:17:32.133 --> 00:17:32.994
Like he literally...

432
00:17:32.994 --> 00:17:35.332
He had beef with certain people.

433
00:17:35.332 --> 00:17:37.054
And so in the media guide, he just like...

434
00:17:37.054 --> 00:17:40.465
They compiled these lists of like top 10 times

435
00:17:40.465 --> 00:17:41.559
for each event.

436
00:17:41.559 --> 00:17:45.882
He just line item, removed them from the record books.

437
00:17:45.882 --> 00:17:48.452
And I think Jason might've been the one just to see it

438
00:17:48.452 --> 00:17:49.422
and realize it.

439
00:17:49.422 --> 00:17:50.774
And he made a stink about it.

440
00:17:50.774 --> 00:17:52.111
Because he was one of the people

441
00:17:52.111 --> 00:17:55.294
who had earned a spot on these lists.

442
00:17:55.294 --> 00:17:58.344
And he had been surreptitiously removed.

443
00:17:58.344 --> 00:17:59.863
Without any explanation.

444
00:17:59.863 --> 00:18:01.633
It's like something out of a Wes Anderson movie.

445
00:18:01.633 --> 00:18:04.335
(Adam laughs)

446
00:18:04.335 --> 00:18:06.433
It's sort of an ego is the enemy.

447
00:18:06.433 --> 00:18:09.584
Skip had a beef, his ego got caught up in it

448
00:18:09.584 --> 00:18:13.425
and he made an error and he ended up getting suspended.

449
00:18:13.425 --> 00:18:17.031
And ultimately that was the end of his career.

450
00:18:17.031 --> 00:18:20.722
And that career was an incredibly successful one.

451
00:18:20.722 --> 00:18:24.361
He lorded over, perhaps one of the most successful

452
00:18:24.361 --> 00:18:25.993
collegiate swim programs

453
00:18:25.993 --> 00:18:27.912
in the history of collegiate swimming.

454
00:18:27.912 --> 00:18:32.641
I mean, he won 26 consecutive (indistinct) Championships.

455
00:18:32.641 --> 00:18:33.603
And he was...

456
00:18:33.603 --> 00:18:35.403
You know, like I had my ups and downs with him.

457
00:18:35.403 --> 00:18:37.333
I have friends that were on the outs with him

458
00:18:37.333 --> 00:18:40.023
and I have friends that loved him.

459
00:18:40.023 --> 00:18:41.873
And my relationship with him is fine.

460
00:18:41.873 --> 00:18:45.290
But he's a crusty, difficult human being.

461
00:18:46.464 --> 00:18:48.435
Plummer has this quote where...

462
00:18:48.435 --> 00:18:50.722
I found it in an article, getting ready for today.

463
00:18:50.722 --> 00:18:53.173
Where he said in reference to Skip

464
00:18:53.173 --> 00:18:55.473
that he was a guy with an ax to grind

465
00:18:55.473 --> 00:18:57.933
who has circumvented common decency.

466
00:18:57.933 --> 00:19:01.536
And I think you can understand why he would feel that way.

467
00:19:01.536 --> 00:19:03.736
But at the same time,

468
00:19:03.736 --> 00:19:07.902
Skip would create these kind of traditions amongst the team

469
00:19:07.902 --> 00:19:09.724
that really congealed us as a unit.

470
00:19:09.724 --> 00:19:12.305
One of those things was called The Pants.

471
00:19:12.305 --> 00:19:16.888
There was this pair of old sweat pants that dated back,

472
00:19:17.834 --> 00:19:18.954
I don't know when.

473
00:19:18.954 --> 00:19:21.343
Like they almost felt like sandpaper.

474
00:19:21.343 --> 00:19:23.735
They were so old, these red sweat pants.

475
00:19:23.735 --> 00:19:27.415
And each year the team would vote on

476
00:19:27.415 --> 00:19:31.354
who would earn the right to wear those pants for the year.

477
00:19:31.354 --> 00:19:32.454
And it was basically the person

478
00:19:32.454 --> 00:19:35.742
who had the most enthusiastic team spirit.

479
00:19:35.742 --> 00:19:37.904
And you would kind of have to wear them, not every day,

480
00:19:37.904 --> 00:19:39.364
but most days.

481
00:19:39.364 --> 00:19:40.526
To meets and stuff?

482
00:19:40.526 --> 00:19:41.614
No, every day,

483
00:19:41.614 --> 00:19:42.447
Every day.

484
00:19:42.447 --> 00:19:45.592
You just wear them for like an entire year.

485
00:19:45.592 --> 00:19:47.092
Oh yeah, here, I found the note.

486
00:19:47.092 --> 00:19:49.175
The pants were from 1967.

487
00:19:50.034 --> 00:19:53.825
From the 1967 (indistinct) Championship

488
00:19:53.825 --> 00:19:56.783
My friend Sam McAdam had them one year,

489
00:19:56.783 --> 00:19:58.042
Mike Reynolds had them one year

490
00:19:58.042 --> 00:20:00.092
and then I had them one year.

491
00:20:00.092 --> 00:20:00.925
You did?

492
00:20:00.925 --> 00:20:02.243
I got to wear the pants for a year.

493
00:20:02.243 --> 00:20:03.076
That's awesome.

494
00:20:03.076 --> 00:20:03.909
But it was stuff like that,

495
00:20:03.909 --> 00:20:06.452
that was sort of created by Skip.

496
00:20:06.452 --> 00:20:08.400
These beautiful little things.

497
00:20:08.400 --> 00:20:10.143
And there was all kinds of other traditions

498
00:20:10.143 --> 00:20:12.544
when we do this annual road trip and all that kind of stuff.

499
00:20:12.544 --> 00:20:14.711
But Skip's tenure ended in

500
00:20:17.563 --> 00:20:22.053
this sort of cloud of ill repute that had no small part

501
00:20:22.053 --> 00:20:25.542
due to Jason and what had gone down.

502
00:20:25.542 --> 00:20:28.372
But all of this to say that Jason will be missed.

503
00:20:28.372 --> 00:20:31.892
he really was like a larger than life character.

504
00:20:31.892 --> 00:20:33.405
And he's gone too soon.

505
00:20:33.405 --> 00:20:34.754
Where was he living?

506
00:20:34.754 --> 00:20:35.587
Was he local here?

507
00:20:35.587 --> 00:20:36.420
In Dallas?

508
00:20:36.420 --> 00:20:38.519
I think he was doing high-end real estate there

509
00:20:38.519 --> 00:20:40.428
or something like that,

510
00:20:40.428 --> 00:20:44.014
but had that kind of Aussi spirit.

511
00:20:44.014 --> 00:20:47.241
Where he would light up a room when you would walk into it.

512
00:20:47.241 --> 00:20:49.449
Sorry about that one.

513
00:20:49.449 --> 00:20:52.587
Here's to the here's to Stoddard and Plummer.

514
00:20:52.587 --> 00:20:54.340
Yeah here's to those guys.

515
00:20:54.340 --> 00:20:56.821
But on the subject of talented athletes,

516
00:20:56.821 --> 00:20:59.181
do we wanna talk about the ascent of the Norwegians?

517
00:20:59.181 --> 00:21:02.586
We must, they've announced themselves.

518
00:21:02.586 --> 00:21:05.288
I don't know if they have been announced.

519
00:21:05.288 --> 00:21:07.609
It's the age of the Norwegians

520
00:21:07.609 --> 00:21:11.407
on the triathlon/Ironman landscape these days.

521
00:21:11.407 --> 00:21:14.248
You know, I would let Norwegians

522
00:21:14.248 --> 00:21:16.168
just run the world personally.

523
00:21:16.168 --> 00:21:17.568
Having met some Norwegians

524
00:21:17.568 --> 00:21:19.469
and seeing how they run their country.

525
00:21:19.469 --> 00:21:20.666
I'm open to-

526
00:21:20.666 --> 00:21:22.128
They make a very nice safe car.

527
00:21:22.128 --> 00:21:24.617
Yeah, I think I'm open to the Norwegians-

528
00:21:24.617 --> 00:21:26.017
Wait, is Volvo Norwegian?

529
00:21:26.017 --> 00:21:27.248
No, it's Swedish?

530
00:21:27.248 --> 00:21:28.269
Yeah.

531
00:21:28.269 --> 00:21:29.567
No Swedish.

