2288
01:34:19.817 --> 01:34:22.595
But let's not forget how young these guys were.

2289
01:34:22.595 --> 01:34:23.515
All of them were young.

2290
01:34:23.515 --> 01:34:25.504
I mean, George was 26, I think.

2291
01:34:25.504 --> 01:34:27.718
And I think Paul and John were 28?

2292
01:34:27.718 --> 01:34:31.318
I think, John was 28 and Paul was 27.

2293
01:34:31.318 --> 01:34:32.151
27?

2294
01:34:32.151 --> 01:34:33.171
That's unbelievable.

2295
01:34:33.171 --> 01:34:34.619
Unbelievable.

2296
01:34:34.619 --> 01:34:37.399
That's unbelievable because, I would have thought,

2297
01:34:37.399 --> 01:34:39.141
even being a fan, "Oh, they broke...

2298
01:34:39.141 --> 01:34:40.671
If you said to me, "How old were they

2299
01:34:40.671 --> 01:34:42.049
when The Beatles broke up?"

2300
01:34:42.049 --> 01:34:43.779
I would've said, "I don't know 35."

2301
01:34:43.779 --> 01:34:44.612
No, no, I knew,

2302
01:34:44.612 --> 01:34:46.918
I knew that they were like 29 or something like that.

2303
01:34:46.918 --> 01:34:47.751
Amazing.

2304
01:34:48.928 --> 01:34:52.928
And I thought it was fun to see Yoko and Linda

2305
01:34:55.077 --> 01:34:58.211
and Ringo's wife who I'm spacing on her name.

2306
01:34:58.211 --> 01:34:59.557
Looking so good.

2307
01:34:59.557 --> 01:35:00.460
And then George's

2308
01:35:00.460 --> 01:35:02.007
Hare Krishna buddies looked great.

2309
01:35:02.007 --> 01:35:04.674
(Both laughing)

2310
01:35:06.635 --> 01:35:07.468
Sorry.

2311
01:35:07.468 --> 01:35:09.217
Yes, George brings in...

2312
01:35:09.217 --> 01:35:12.505
There's a couple Hare Krishna monks that sit in the corner

2313
01:35:12.505 --> 01:35:14.635
at what's the studio called again?

2314
01:35:14.635 --> 01:35:16.505
It has a very British name, like Wickenham

2315
01:35:16.505 --> 01:35:17.477
or something like that.

2316
01:35:17.477 --> 01:35:18.394
Twickenham.

2317
01:35:19.706 --> 01:35:22.605
And then Yoko who pulls up a chair

2318
01:35:22.605 --> 01:35:24.408
and kind of sits right with the group.

2319
01:35:24.408 --> 01:35:27.158
And what you notice over time is,

2320
01:35:28.527 --> 01:35:32.785
although there is something a little bit presumptuous

2321
01:35:32.785 --> 01:35:36.686
about her inserting herself right into the middle

2322
01:35:36.686 --> 01:35:38.019
of this dynamic.

2323
01:35:38.886 --> 01:35:41.977
She never says a word, she's not bothering anyone.

2324
01:35:41.977 --> 01:35:46.060
And it really upends that narrative that Yoko Ono

2325
01:35:47.289 --> 01:35:48.145
broke up the band.

2326
01:35:48.145 --> 01:35:51.791
It's hard to make that argument after you observed this.

2327
01:35:51.791 --> 01:35:53.958
I said that to a friend who has not seen this yet,

2328
01:35:53.958 --> 01:35:56.135
but who said the, Let it Be Documentary

2329
01:35:56.135 --> 01:35:57.555
is kind of the opposite of that.

2330
01:35:57.555 --> 01:35:59.252
And I didn't see the Let it Be Documentary.

2331
01:35:59.252 --> 01:36:00.129
Did you see that?

2332
01:36:00.129 --> 01:36:00.962
Or it makes it seem...

2333
01:36:00.962 --> 01:36:02.657
It kind of plays up the division's a bit more.

2334
01:36:02.657 --> 01:36:05.225
They are angry at each other that Yoko

2335
01:36:05.225 --> 01:36:06.598
is kind of getting in the way.

2336
01:36:06.598 --> 01:36:08.827
It doesn't seem that way in this particular setting.

2337
01:36:08.827 --> 01:36:11.347
In part two Paul kind of addresses it directly

2338
01:36:11.347 --> 01:36:15.036
and kind of puts those rumors to bed.

2339
01:36:15.036 --> 01:36:19.105
I mean, they acknowledge, "Oh, well, Yoko's here.

2340
01:36:19.105 --> 01:36:19.938
What does that mean?"

2341
01:36:19.938 --> 01:36:22.016
And they talk about it.

2342
01:36:22.016 --> 01:36:24.683
I think the bigger issue is that

2343
01:36:26.736 --> 01:36:29.705
when Brian Epstein passed away,

2344
01:36:29.705 --> 01:36:31.656
they didn't have like a team captain.

2345
01:36:31.656 --> 01:36:33.496
There was a power vacuum.

2346
01:36:33.496 --> 01:36:34.655
Explain who Brian Epstein was.

2347
01:36:34.655 --> 01:36:37.885
So he was like their manager from the get-go

2348
01:36:37.885 --> 01:36:39.517
and somebody that they...

2349
01:36:39.517 --> 01:36:40.487
You could tell in the movie,

2350
01:36:40.487 --> 01:36:43.137
how much they loved and trusted and respected this guy.

2351
01:36:43.137 --> 01:36:44.366
They call him Mr. Epstein.

2352
01:36:44.366 --> 01:36:45.199
He was their dad.

2353
01:36:45.199 --> 01:36:46.032
He put them in suits.

2354
01:36:46.032 --> 01:36:47.075
He told them what to dress.

2355
01:36:47.075 --> 01:36:48.817
How they would and they would do it.

2356
01:36:48.817 --> 01:36:50.077
And then when he's gone,

2357
01:36:50.077 --> 01:36:54.648
there's a power vacuum that I think I would intuit

2358
01:36:54.648 --> 01:36:56.407
that Paul fills.

2359
01:36:56.407 --> 01:36:58.849
When you watch this documentary, you realize

2360
01:36:58.849 --> 01:37:00.917
Paul's really the leader.

2361
01:37:00.917 --> 01:37:02.656
Nothing gets done without Paul.

2362
01:37:02.656 --> 01:37:06.168
He's the only one who has any interest in any organization

2363
01:37:06.168 --> 01:37:07.835
or productivity here.

2364
01:37:07.835 --> 01:37:10.499
Cause it's a bunch of artists and they think,

2365
01:37:10.499 --> 01:37:12.850
and do things differently.

2366
01:37:12.850 --> 01:37:15.469
And he's really trying hard in the best way

2367
01:37:15.469 --> 01:37:19.238
to try to drive this train forward and get something done.

2368
01:37:19.238 --> 01:37:22.831
But without Paul, nothing gets done.

2369
01:37:22.831 --> 01:37:23.870
That's clear.

2370
01:37:23.870 --> 01:37:25.351
But without Epstein,

2371
01:37:25.351 --> 01:37:30.351
I think it created this weird dynamic where Paul had to fill

2372
01:37:30.997 --> 01:37:33.198
a certain power vacuum,

2373
01:37:33.198 --> 01:37:35.406
but then that can lead to resentment

2374
01:37:35.406 --> 01:37:36.528
and all kinds of other things.

2375
01:37:36.528 --> 01:37:39.050
He would allow Paul to be Paul with someone else

2376
01:37:39.050 --> 01:37:40.258
(indistinct chatter)

2377
01:37:40.258 --> 01:37:43.777
George could have talked to Brian or John could.

2378
01:37:43.777 --> 01:37:45.940
Because you need Paul as the engine.

2379
01:37:45.940 --> 01:37:46.992
He's always gonna be the engine.

2380
01:37:46.992 --> 01:37:49.409
He is the conductor in a way.

2381
01:37:51.647 --> 01:37:53.915
But to see them all together and make their magic

2382
01:37:53.915 --> 01:37:56.206
and to have George come in with a fully realized song,

2383
01:37:56.206 --> 01:37:58.987
he wrote in an hour or at home the night before.

2384
01:37:58.987 --> 01:38:01.297
And then have Paul just invent them in front of your eyes.

2385
01:38:01.297 --> 01:38:03.296
Yeah, they're like, "I had this idea last night."

2386
01:38:03.296 --> 01:38:06.827
And then they sit down and it's 70% of the song is there

2387
01:38:06.827 --> 01:38:07.660
and they're working it out

2388
01:38:07.660 --> 01:38:08.530
and they're trying different things.

2389
01:38:08.530 --> 01:38:10.478
And because you know the song so well, you're like,

2390
01:38:10.478 --> 01:38:12.977
"No, no, don't do that, it's like this."

2391
01:38:12.977 --> 01:38:16.239
And how about the original words for don't let me down.

2392
01:38:16.239 --> 01:38:20.378
It was like on the road to (indistinct) or on the road...

2393
01:38:20.378 --> 01:38:22.461
Something like that.

2394
01:38:22.461 --> 01:38:25.494
That John was singing and playing.

2395
01:38:25.494 --> 01:38:28.559
And then he did one, he brought in a vinyl demo

2396
01:38:28.559 --> 01:38:30.489
of Across the Universe for them to listen to,

2397
01:38:30.489 --> 01:38:31.757
and then they all got into it.

2398
01:38:31.757 --> 01:38:34.198
I mean like, so the different approaches that they take,

2399
01:38:34.198 --> 01:38:35.957
like he actually did a whole demo

2400
01:38:35.957 --> 01:38:37.844
of Across the Universe for people.

2401
01:38:37.844 --> 01:38:40.496
And there's this other thing I wanna get out.

2402
01:38:40.496 --> 01:38:42.574
Cause I've read...

2403
01:38:42.574 --> 01:38:44.475
I did a little bit of reading on the show

2404
01:38:44.475 --> 01:38:45.347
before watching it.

2405
01:38:45.347 --> 01:38:48.745
And like you I stopped whenever a spoiler kind of came up.

2406
01:38:48.745 --> 01:38:51.947
But one thing that I noticed a theme of was Let it Be,

2407
01:38:51.947 --> 01:38:54.777
is considered this lesser album.

2408
01:38:54.777 --> 01:38:59.067
And partly because of Phil Spector, kind of doing some...

2409
01:38:59.067 --> 01:39:01.685
You know, desk fuckwit stuff with it later.

2410
01:39:01.685 --> 01:39:05.518
But in reality, there's five classics on this.

2411
01:39:06.665 --> 01:39:09.099
On this album, Across the Universe, Let it Be,

2412
01:39:09.099 --> 01:39:11.021
Get Back, Don't Let Me Down, and Two of Us,

2413
01:39:11.021 --> 01:39:12.591
which I think is great.

2414
01:39:12.591 --> 01:39:14.587
And then you have George, some of George's songs,

2415
01:39:14.587 --> 01:39:15.821
which are great.

2416
01:39:15.821 --> 01:39:19.371
And I mean to me, I love the album,

2417
01:39:19.371 --> 01:39:20.768
but it is considered a lesser album,

2418
01:39:20.768 --> 01:39:22.549
which I don't quite understand.

2419
01:39:22.549 --> 01:39:24.299
That's interesting.

2420
01:39:25.741 --> 01:39:27.942
Can we talk about Glen Johns.

2421
01:39:27.942 --> 01:39:29.179
Glen Johns.

2422
01:39:29.179 --> 01:39:31.492
What a superstar this guy is.

2423
01:39:31.492 --> 01:39:32.325
Wait Glen John's.

2424
01:39:32.325 --> 01:39:33.158
In the background-

2425
01:39:33.158 --> 01:39:34.241
The hammer?

2426
01:39:35.366 --> 01:39:36.498
No, not that guy.

2427
01:39:36.498 --> 01:39:37.687
(Adam laughing)

2428
01:39:37.687 --> 01:39:38.910
Glenn Johns was their

2429
01:39:38.910 --> 01:39:43.609
sound engineer/producer/silent Beetle member.

2430
01:39:43.609 --> 01:39:47.127
Who, first of all, always has the best outfit.

2431
01:39:47.127 --> 01:39:48.686
That guy is just rocking it.

2432
01:39:48.686 --> 01:39:51.064
Comes in with the scarf and the crazy sunglasses,

2433
01:39:51.064 --> 01:39:54.325
and a alligator skin jacket and the whole thing.

2434
01:39:54.325 --> 01:39:56.894
And is in charge of creating the sound for all of this.

2435
01:39:56.894 --> 01:39:59.079
There's one song, I can't remember

2436
01:39:59.079 --> 01:40:01.627
because I watched part one a couple of days ago

2437
01:40:01.627 --> 01:40:02.559
at this point.

2438
01:40:02.559 --> 01:40:05.537
Where they have a hurdle with one of the songs

2439
01:40:05.537 --> 01:40:06.730
they're working out.

2440
01:40:06.730 --> 01:40:08.605
And they keep going back and forth and it's not working.

2441
01:40:08.605 --> 01:40:10.146
And that was one of those situations where I was like,

2442
01:40:10.146 --> 01:40:11.527
"No, not that, not that."

2443
01:40:11.527 --> 01:40:15.678
And Glen says one thing and it solves the problem.

2444
01:40:15.678 --> 01:40:16.637
Yeah, right.

2445
01:40:16.637 --> 01:40:17.470
You remember that?

2446
01:40:17.470 --> 01:40:18.303
Yeah, I do remember that.

2447
01:40:18.303 --> 01:40:20.967
Then he's really kind of this unsung hero

2448
01:40:20.967 --> 01:40:22.368
and the whole thing behind the scenes.

2449
01:40:22.368 --> 01:40:24.267
Yeah, no, I do remember that.

2450
01:40:24.267 --> 01:40:25.988
And just an absolute legend.

2451
01:40:25.988 --> 01:40:27.088
Did he produce the record?

2452
01:40:27.088 --> 01:40:27.921
I don't know.

2453
01:40:27.921 --> 01:40:28.936
Jason, do you know.

2454
01:40:28.936 --> 01:40:31.410
Glen is just an absolute legend though, right?

2455
01:40:31.410 --> 01:40:32.243
Yeah.

2456
01:40:32.243 --> 01:40:35.096
Zeplin Stones, Who, Let it Be.

2457
01:40:35.096 --> 01:40:36.832
He comes up with the declining line.

2458
01:40:36.832 --> 01:40:38.911
On Let it Be right, exactly.

2459
01:40:38.911 --> 01:40:39.744
Sorry Jason.

2460
01:40:39.744 --> 01:40:41.222
(indistinct chatter)

2461
01:40:41.222 --> 01:40:42.809
Jason just gave me a dirty look.

2462
01:40:42.809 --> 01:40:43.642
Getting the stink eye?

2463
01:40:43.642 --> 01:40:45.715
From Jason, how dare you?

2464
01:40:45.715 --> 01:40:46.559
Jason, I love you.

2465
01:40:46.559 --> 01:40:49.700
The film comes out and Jason just basically sits

2466
01:40:49.700 --> 01:40:52.672
and watches the whole thing in one sitting

2467
01:40:52.672 --> 01:40:55.025
extensively, right?

2468
01:40:55.025 --> 01:40:57.351
Jason didn't get dressed that day.

2469
01:40:57.351 --> 01:40:58.736
He just sat there.

2470
01:40:58.736 --> 01:41:01.739
My head was exploding when I was watching part one.

2471
01:41:01.739 --> 01:41:03.738
I just couldn't believe what I was witnessing.

2472
01:41:03.738 --> 01:41:07.071
I was so grateful that this thing exists

2473
01:41:09.245 --> 01:41:10.767
and that we got to have...

2474
01:41:10.767 --> 01:41:14.020
Be able to witness this thing happening.

2475
01:41:14.020 --> 01:41:17.418
I just thought it's absolutely magical.

2476
01:41:17.418 --> 01:41:19.842
Like he said, maybe it's not for everyone,

2477
01:41:19.842 --> 01:41:22.750
but for me, I was like, just injected into my veins.

2478
01:41:22.750 --> 01:41:25.458
How did Peter Jackson get ahold of this stuff?

2479
01:41:25.458 --> 01:41:26.329
I don't know.

2480
01:41:26.329 --> 01:41:29.681
Yeah, I haven't read up on that, but I know

2481
01:41:29.681 --> 01:41:32.961
it's taken him quite a long time to get this thing together.

2482
01:41:32.961 --> 01:41:34.539
And there's been a team of people working on it

2483
01:41:34.539 --> 01:41:35.600
for a while.

2484
01:41:35.600 --> 01:41:36.459
Well he lives in middle earth.

2485
01:41:36.459 --> 01:41:38.200
It's hard to get the footage there.

2486
01:41:38.200 --> 01:41:40.527
Well the footage was sitting in a vault somewhere,

2487
01:41:40.527 --> 01:41:42.194
owned by Apple Corp.

2488
01:41:43.159 --> 01:41:44.600
And somehow he got permission.

2489
01:41:44.600 --> 01:41:45.847
They never used it for anything.

2490
01:41:45.847 --> 01:41:49.741
The intention was that there was gonna be this documentary.

2491
01:41:49.741 --> 01:41:51.201
That for whatever reason, they decided,

2492
01:41:51.201 --> 01:41:52.170
"Well, there's nothing here."

2493
01:41:52.170 --> 01:41:54.161
It's funny, cause in the thing they're like,

2494
01:41:54.161 --> 01:41:56.070
"How's the documentary going?"

2495
01:41:56.070 --> 01:41:58.280
And the director, who's a character in his own right.

2496
01:41:58.280 --> 01:42:00.082
With a cigar and the whole thing

2497
01:42:00.082 --> 01:42:01.108
he's like, I don't know...

2498
01:42:01.108 --> 01:42:03.330
He's like, "I don't think we have a documentary."

2499
01:42:03.330 --> 01:42:04.459
When things start going sideways.

2500
01:42:04.459 --> 01:42:08.792
And John's like, "What do you mean it's taking off?"

2501
01:42:09.754 --> 01:42:11.410
Now it's getting good.

2502
01:42:11.410 --> 01:42:12.391
It's pretty funny.

2503
01:42:12.391 --> 01:42:13.419
I want the guy that-

2504
01:42:13.419 --> 01:42:16.661
And it looks like yesterday.

2505
01:42:16.661 --> 01:42:18.807
I don't know how much cleaning up of the footage

2506
01:42:18.807 --> 01:42:20.156
had to be done.

2507
01:42:20.156 --> 01:42:22.472
But it literally looks like yesterday.

2508
01:42:22.472 --> 01:42:24.087
And the fact that they have...

2509
01:42:24.087 --> 01:42:24.950
There's like a boom mic

2510
01:42:24.950 --> 01:42:26.759
over to all these private conversations

2511
01:42:26.759 --> 01:42:28.410
that they were able to capture.

2512
01:42:28.410 --> 01:42:30.149
Yesterday.

2513
01:42:30.149 --> 01:42:31.780
I like the hammer swinger.

2514
01:42:31.780 --> 01:42:34.198
The hammer swinger who is a close talker as well.

2515
01:42:34.198 --> 01:42:38.070
No, he's become a meme on Twitter too.

2516
01:42:38.070 --> 01:42:39.408
Cause he's such a hero.

2517
01:42:39.408 --> 01:42:42.490
He gets to bang the hammer on the Advil.

2518
01:42:42.490 --> 01:42:43.552
So chime in on the song.

2519
01:42:43.552 --> 01:42:45.089
Jason, what's that guy's name?

2520
01:42:45.089 --> 01:42:47.607
That was their road manager.

2521
01:42:47.607 --> 01:42:49.558
The road manager, right, yeah.

2522
01:42:49.558 --> 01:42:51.543
He is a close talker, is he not?

2523
01:42:51.543 --> 01:42:53.427
Yeah.

2524
01:42:53.427 --> 01:42:55.319
He's great.

2525
01:42:55.319 --> 01:42:56.433
If you see the other footage of the Beatles

2526
01:42:56.433 --> 01:42:58.941
early 60s playing in DC.

2527
01:42:58.941 --> 01:43:01.481
(indistinct) is this the guy that comes out on stage

2528
01:43:01.481 --> 01:43:04.462
and spins Ringo's drum riser.

2529
01:43:04.462 --> 01:43:07.152
So the crowd on the other side...

2530
01:43:07.152 --> 01:43:08.879
Is one of the guys from the original.

2531
01:43:08.879 --> 01:43:09.712
That's cool.

2532
01:43:09.712 --> 01:43:11.770
And what's the director's name is?

2533
01:43:11.770 --> 01:43:13.035
Michael Lindsay Hogg.

2534
01:43:13.035 --> 01:43:14.153
Lindsey Hogg.

2535
01:43:14.153 --> 01:43:17.322
So he's the guy with the cigar and it's so funny,

2536
01:43:17.322 --> 01:43:18.321
Tyco YTD tweeted.

2537
01:43:18.321 --> 01:43:21.120
He's like, "This guy again, trying to get them to go

2538
01:43:21.120 --> 01:43:22.962
to Libya for the concert."

2539
01:43:22.962 --> 01:43:25.761
He's like, "They don't wanna do that."

2540
01:43:25.761 --> 01:43:26.770
Pretty funny.

2541
01:43:26.770 --> 01:43:29.827
I just love watching them in that room together.

2542
01:43:29.827 --> 01:43:31.945
How about when Linda comes in

2543
01:43:31.945 --> 01:43:34.862
and starts taking pictures and then you see the pictures

2544
01:43:34.862 --> 01:43:35.861
that she's taking.

2545
01:43:35.861 --> 01:43:36.694
Amazing.

2546
01:43:36.694 --> 01:43:37.527
It's unbelievable.

2547
01:43:37.527 --> 01:43:38.360
Soft focus, and-

2548
01:43:38.360 --> 01:43:39.485
I know.

2549
01:43:39.485 --> 01:43:41.224
I mean, I get emotional just thinking about it

2550
01:43:41.224 --> 01:43:43.459
and I'm like, "Why am I so emotional about this?"

2551
01:43:43.459 --> 01:43:45.294
But it's music history.

2552
01:43:45.294 --> 01:43:46.832
It's beautiful, it's okay to be emotional.

2553
01:43:46.832 --> 01:43:48.803
It is music history.

2554
01:43:48.803 --> 01:43:51.386
It really is a beautiful thing.

2555
01:43:52.833 --> 01:43:54.173
What else can we say?

2556
01:43:54.173 --> 01:43:55.340
About that?

2557
01:43:55.340 --> 01:43:57.551
About the movie.

2558
01:43:57.551 --> 01:43:59.693
I mean, I still have two episodes to go.

2559
01:43:59.693 --> 01:44:00.592
So I'm pretty stoked.

2560
01:44:00.592 --> 01:44:02.004
I'm looking forward to seeing what happens.

2561
01:44:02.004 --> 01:44:04.051
I saw the end of the first episode,

2562
01:44:04.051 --> 01:44:05.201
I was like, "Ooh."

2563
01:44:05.201 --> 01:44:07.868
I got to see Ringo's fat estate.

2564
01:44:09.750 --> 01:44:12.500
Ringo is a hero to many, I think.

2565
01:44:14.841 --> 01:44:17.540
Just his disposition throughout the whole thing

2566
01:44:17.540 --> 01:44:18.690
is so laudable.

2567
01:44:18.690 --> 01:44:20.140
It is.

2568
01:44:20.140 --> 01:44:20.973
Team player.

2569
01:44:20.973 --> 01:44:22.253
He's a team player.

2570
01:44:22.253 --> 01:44:23.180
And he's chill.

2571
01:44:23.180 --> 01:44:24.013
Here's the thing-

2572
01:44:24.013 --> 01:44:26.094
The others are pretending to be spiritual,

2573
01:44:26.094 --> 01:44:27.334
but Ringo is spiritual.

2574
01:44:27.334 --> 01:44:29.894
You've got your George issue and that blah, blah, blah.

2575
01:44:29.894 --> 01:44:30.727
I never did though.

2576
01:44:30.727 --> 01:44:32.935
But here's the thing, these guys are artists.

2577
01:44:32.935 --> 01:44:34.094
They don't function like...

2578
01:44:34.094 --> 01:44:36.432
It's like who cares, who showed up on time?

2579
01:44:36.432 --> 01:44:39.115
Ultimately all that matters is how great the album is.

2580
01:44:39.115 --> 01:44:39.948
Obviously-

2581
01:44:39.948 --> 01:44:40.781
Ringo cares, okay.

2582
01:44:40.781 --> 01:44:43.515
Obviously all of those personality peccadilloes

2583
01:44:43.515 --> 01:44:46.552
can either make the band grow closer together

2584
01:44:46.552 --> 01:44:48.063
or split them apart.

2585
01:44:48.063 --> 01:44:49.055
So it's not...

2586
01:44:49.055 --> 01:44:50.736
It is important.

2587
01:44:50.736 --> 01:44:54.341
But what you see is Paul being this guy

2588
01:44:54.341 --> 01:44:55.473
who shouldered the mantle

2589
01:44:55.473 --> 01:44:59.060
of being responsible for getting stuff done.

2590
01:44:59.060 --> 01:45:02.070
You have John who's consistently coming in late.

2591
01:45:02.070 --> 01:45:04.320
But in good spirits.

2592
01:45:04.320 --> 01:45:06.822
And it takes John a Long time to get warmed up.

2593
01:45:06.822 --> 01:45:08.310
But once he gets warmed up

2594
01:45:08.310 --> 01:45:10.961
and he gets in sync with Paul, magic happens.

2595
01:45:10.961 --> 01:45:13.871
And then you see them telling jokes and screwing around

2596
01:45:13.871 --> 01:45:15.700
and having fun.

2597
01:45:15.700 --> 01:45:17.352
And you think, "Well, that's not productive."

2598
01:45:17.352 --> 01:45:20.518
But that's important for the whole creative process.

2599
01:45:20.518 --> 01:45:22.890
You can't have one without the other

2600
01:45:22.890 --> 01:45:26.259
and artists, they're sensitive beings.

2601
01:45:26.259 --> 01:45:27.638
And they don't function.

2602
01:45:27.638 --> 01:45:30.492
Rationality isn't necessarily a priority.

2603
01:45:30.492 --> 01:45:31.359
I agree.

2604
01:45:31.359 --> 01:45:34.729
Look, I'm not saying it matters.

2605
01:45:34.729 --> 01:45:35.861
You don't have to be on time,

2606
01:45:35.861 --> 01:45:37.591
especially if you're John Lennon.

2607
01:45:37.591 --> 01:45:39.098
So here's the thing,

2608
01:45:39.098 --> 01:45:40.601
George didn't have to show up on time,

2609
01:45:40.601 --> 01:45:43.112
but he damn sure had to get there before John Lennon.

2610
01:45:43.112 --> 01:45:45.451
(Adam laughing)

2611
01:45:45.451 --> 01:45:47.361
And Ringo couldn't be last.

2612
01:45:47.361 --> 01:45:48.281
Ringo had to be first.

2613
01:45:48.281 --> 01:45:50.799
So yes, it doesn't matter, but it does matter.

2614
01:45:50.799 --> 01:45:51.892
There is still standing.

2615
01:45:51.892 --> 01:45:53.140
There is still the hierarchy.

2616
01:45:53.140 --> 01:45:54.341
John could be last.

2617
01:45:54.341 --> 01:45:56.114
Paul could've been last too.

2618
01:45:56.114 --> 01:45:58.141
But he would never be last cause he knows

2619
01:45:58.141 --> 01:45:59.810
someone has to be there first.

2620
01:45:59.810 --> 01:46:01.670
And if he gets there, he knows Ringo would get there.

2621
01:46:01.670 --> 01:46:03.281
So then there's waiting for two people.

2622
01:46:03.281 --> 01:46:05.382
I mean, that's just part of the workman like thing.

2623
01:46:05.382 --> 01:46:07.490
That's what you're seeing in this, which I like.

2624
01:46:07.490 --> 01:46:11.512
I'm not criticizing John for being late at all or George,

2625
01:46:11.512 --> 01:46:13.530
but I just think it is interesting.

2626
01:46:13.530 --> 01:46:14.479
George definitely was...

2627
01:46:14.479 --> 01:46:17.329
He was late, but he wasn't as late as John.

2628
01:46:17.329 --> 01:46:18.441
I got you.

2629
01:46:18.441 --> 01:46:21.410
Also, I appreciate all the little details.

2630
01:46:21.410 --> 01:46:25.936
Like the roadies who come in and give them tea.

2631
01:46:25.936 --> 01:46:27.450
There's always toast.

2632
01:46:27.450 --> 01:46:28.283
I love the tea and toast.

2633
01:46:28.283 --> 01:46:29.732
Toast and mermalade.

2634
01:46:29.732 --> 01:46:34.149
And the sound is for the documentary is Alan Parsons.

2635
01:46:35.100 --> 01:46:35.933
Amazing.

2636
01:46:35.933 --> 01:46:37.891
It's unbelievable.

2637
01:46:37.891 --> 01:46:38.724
All right.

2638
01:46:38.724 --> 01:46:41.661
Well, so watching that alongside this other doc

2639
01:46:41.661 --> 01:46:42.561
that you turned me on to.

2640
01:46:42.561 --> 01:46:45.480
Todd Haynes directed doc, the velvet under...

2641
01:46:45.480 --> 01:46:47.030
It's called Velvet Goldmine, is that right?

2642
01:46:47.030 --> 01:46:47.870
Yes.

2643
01:46:47.870 --> 01:46:51.120
No Velvet Goldmine was another movie that Todd Haynes did.

2644
01:46:51.120 --> 01:46:52.931
That was a narrative.

2645
01:46:52.931 --> 01:46:54.850
So this is Velvet Underground Doc.

2646
01:46:54.850 --> 01:46:55.929
It doesn't really have it...

2647
01:46:55.929 --> 01:46:57.150
I think it's just Velvet Underground.

2648
01:46:57.150 --> 01:46:58.489
Velvet Underground.

2649
01:46:58.489 --> 01:47:02.822
And it's kind of like a partner to this other thing.

2650
01:47:04.049 --> 01:47:06.321
Whereas Peter Jackson's got the invisible hand

2651
01:47:06.321 --> 01:47:08.008
doing incredible work.

2652
01:47:08.008 --> 01:47:10.579
Todd Haynes is definitely mixing it up.

2653
01:47:10.579 --> 01:47:13.460
You see his hand, but it's remarkable.

2654
01:47:13.460 --> 01:47:16.909
And he's using incredible filmmaking techniques

2655
01:47:16.909 --> 01:47:20.522
to create this hypnotic split screen tiled,

2656
01:47:20.522 --> 01:47:25.439
artistic vision that not only gets you into Lou Reed's mind

2657
01:47:26.842 --> 01:47:29.639
and John Cale and (indistinct) Morrison and Mo Tucker

2658
01:47:29.639 --> 01:47:33.963
and all the entire band Nico, but also the factory itself.

2659
01:47:33.963 --> 01:47:36.422
And Andy Warhol who managed the man.

2660
01:47:36.422 --> 01:47:40.003
And man, I want you to talk about this

2661
01:47:40.003 --> 01:47:41.070
and I've got some things to say,

2662
01:47:41.070 --> 01:47:45.002
but to see the two of them back to back,

2663
01:47:45.002 --> 01:47:49.329
it really set my mind and my brain into such a lovely swirl.

2664
01:47:49.329 --> 01:47:51.193
It really kind of got me thinking

2665
01:47:51.193 --> 01:47:53.139
about the nature of creativity in general.

2666
01:47:53.139 --> 01:47:54.632
Yeah, I mean these movies,

2667
01:47:54.632 --> 01:47:58.762
I think go very well together for that reason.

2668
01:47:58.762 --> 01:48:03.762
They're both time capsules about very romantic periods.

2669
01:48:03.862 --> 01:48:06.248
I mean, I have a irrational romantic attachment

2670
01:48:06.248 --> 01:48:10.291
with New York City during the period of Warhol's Factory.

2671
01:48:10.291 --> 01:48:14.988
And so this is right up my alley, this kind of movie.

2672
01:48:14.988 --> 01:48:18.392
But I think what's interesting about both of these movies

2673
01:48:18.392 --> 01:48:20.739
and in particular, the Velvet Underground movie

2674
01:48:20.739 --> 01:48:24.822
is that most rock documentaries are talking heads

2675
01:48:26.110 --> 01:48:28.719
reflecting upon the past.

2676
01:48:28.719 --> 01:48:31.282
Whereas both of these movies really immerse you

2677
01:48:31.282 --> 01:48:33.163
in a present moment.

2678
01:48:33.163 --> 01:48:34.159
They're, experiential.

2679
01:48:34.159 --> 01:48:37.809
You're with the Beatles when they're recording this album.

2680
01:48:37.809 --> 01:48:40.642
And similarly you're with the Velvet Underground,

2681
01:48:40.642 --> 01:48:42.671
because they have a lot of footage of them

2682
01:48:42.671 --> 01:48:44.450
in their Genesis phase.

2683
01:48:44.450 --> 01:48:48.003
So you're experiencing that band at its inception

2684
01:48:48.003 --> 01:48:50.650
and living with them as they try to kind of

2685
01:48:50.650 --> 01:48:53.410
put the pieces together and create something.

2686
01:48:53.410 --> 01:48:54.911
Of course there are some talking heads

2687
01:48:54.911 --> 01:48:55.744
in the Velvet Underground.

2688
01:48:55.744 --> 01:48:57.173
It's spice, it's not-

2689
01:48:57.173 --> 01:49:01.131
It doesn't form the architecture of the whole movie.

2690
01:49:01.131 --> 01:49:04.141
And the movie is crafted so that it feels

2691
01:49:04.141 --> 01:49:06.724
like an Andy Warhol experience.

2692
01:49:07.850 --> 01:49:10.410
And he uses much of Warhol's film.

2693
01:49:10.410 --> 01:49:13.606
Andy Warhol would put a camera on somebody's face

2694
01:49:13.606 --> 01:49:16.518
like Lou Reed and just let it roll for like an hour.

2695
01:49:16.518 --> 01:49:19.643
And Lou would just look into the camera blankly.

2696
01:49:19.643 --> 01:49:22.280
And he uses the split-screen in the movie

2697
01:49:22.280 --> 01:49:25.802
to use those clips, a lot of black and white.

2698
01:49:25.802 --> 01:49:30.248
So you feel like you're in downtown New York City

2699
01:49:30.248 --> 01:49:32.591
in the early 1970s.

2700
01:49:32.591 --> 01:49:35.496
And you can like taste the grit.

2701
01:49:35.496 --> 01:49:37.200
And you can feel the energy.

2702
01:49:37.200 --> 01:49:39.870
Because that was a period of time in which

2703
01:49:39.870 --> 01:49:40.880
there was this flourish

2704
01:49:40.880 --> 01:49:45.162
of interesting alternative creativity that was happening.

2705
01:49:45.162 --> 01:49:46.311
That was very special.

2706
01:49:46.311 --> 01:49:50.241
That birthed like so many amazing artists.

2707
01:49:50.241 --> 01:49:54.600
Not just in music, but in art and film theater, et cetera.

2708
01:49:54.600 --> 01:49:57.279
And I think that's why I have such an obsession

2709
01:49:57.279 --> 01:49:58.280
with this period of time.

2710
01:49:58.280 --> 01:50:00.292
And certainly the Velvet Underground occupies

2711
01:50:00.292 --> 01:50:04.459
a very special place in that kind of conversation.

2712
01:50:06.190 --> 01:50:08.181
Interesting, because I wasn't...

2713
01:50:08.181 --> 01:50:10.600
The context of Velvet Underground I had all off.

2714
01:50:10.600 --> 01:50:12.050
I thought they were early 70's.

2715
01:50:12.050 --> 01:50:14.328
I didn't realize they were like late 60's.

2716
01:50:14.328 --> 01:50:19.139
And this antidote to the kind of flower power.

2717
01:50:19.139 --> 01:50:20.127
They were the counter-culture within the counter-culture.

2718
01:50:20.127 --> 01:50:21.738
They were like a fuck you tot that.

2719
01:50:21.738 --> 01:50:24.725
They were the first punk band, I think you could say.

2720
01:50:24.725 --> 01:50:25.558
I mean, I would say it.

2721
01:50:25.558 --> 01:50:28.658
You would look at it and it's like the sound

2722
01:50:28.658 --> 01:50:32.991
on the dark side of the doors or like the drone and-

2723
01:50:33.937 --> 01:50:36.166
But it was more performance art than punk.

2724
01:50:36.166 --> 01:50:38.918
What was fascinating was learning about

2725
01:50:38.918 --> 01:50:41.463
how it all came together.

2726
01:50:41.463 --> 01:50:45.963
Which begins really with Kale and his Welsh upbringing

2727
01:50:47.759 --> 01:50:50.348
as this classical musician

2728
01:50:50.348 --> 01:50:55.279
who got intrigued with experimental music that was

2729
01:50:55.279 --> 01:50:56.286
going on in New York City.

2730
01:50:56.286 --> 01:50:58.748
Moves out there for the purpose, sole purpose really

2731
01:50:58.748 --> 01:51:02.837
of kind of spending time with these artists

2732
01:51:02.837 --> 01:51:06.079
who were really pushing the boundary of what even music was

2733
01:51:06.079 --> 01:51:07.777
just playing around with tone, right?

2734
01:51:07.777 --> 01:51:08.610
Yeah.

2735
01:51:08.610 --> 01:51:10.959
Just how long can we play this tone

2736
01:51:10.959 --> 01:51:13.581
and immerse ourselves in this sound?

2737
01:51:13.581 --> 01:51:15.180
Just really pushing the envelope

2738
01:51:15.180 --> 01:51:17.602
of what people would tolerate

2739
01:51:17.602 --> 01:51:19.842
and what that experience would look like.

2740
01:51:19.842 --> 01:51:21.061
The son of a coal miner.

2741
01:51:21.061 --> 01:51:23.940
Yeah, amazing story, this guy.

2742
01:51:23.940 --> 01:51:26.912
But also fascinating is that so many

2743
01:51:26.912 --> 01:51:28.941
of these important people end up

2744
01:51:28.941 --> 01:51:32.361
in the same apartment building at 56 Ludlow.

2745
01:51:32.361 --> 01:51:34.911
Which much like the Chelsea hotel

2746
01:51:34.911 --> 01:51:39.538
becomes this ground zero for all this incredible art

2747
01:51:39.538 --> 01:51:40.642
that comes out of it.

2748
01:51:40.642 --> 01:51:43.960
And it was that kind of proximity of these artists

2749
01:51:43.960 --> 01:51:45.413
and the free time that they had.

2750
01:51:45.413 --> 01:51:46.263
Cause I think it caused...

2751
01:51:46.263 --> 01:51:49.142
They all live there cause it costs like $25 a month.

2752
01:51:49.142 --> 01:51:51.214
So they didn't really have to work that much

2753
01:51:51.214 --> 01:51:52.335
to pay their bills.

2754
01:51:52.335 --> 01:51:54.812
And they could fuck around all day

2755
01:51:54.812 --> 01:51:57.991
experimenting with tone and sound and all of these things.

2756
01:51:57.991 --> 01:51:58.824
Get their $10,000.

2757
01:51:58.824 --> 01:52:00.472
Do these kinds of salons where,

2758
01:52:00.472 --> 01:52:02.792
all the hipsters would come together

2759
01:52:02.792 --> 01:52:05.937
and they would indulge themselves with whatever

2760
01:52:05.937 --> 01:52:09.281
artistic kind of thread that they were pulling at the time.

2761
01:52:09.281 --> 01:52:11.432
But the truth is it might not have gone anywhere

2762
01:52:11.432 --> 01:52:14.020
because they were playing this cafe

2763
01:52:14.020 --> 01:52:15.855
and that actually people hated it.

2764
01:52:15.855 --> 01:52:18.622
Except for one person who was in with the factory,

2765
01:52:18.622 --> 01:52:19.965
I forget her name.

2766
01:52:19.965 --> 01:52:24.563
And she sees it and tells Andy that they have to go there

2767
01:52:24.563 --> 01:52:25.425
and they need to see it.

2768
01:52:25.425 --> 01:52:26.336
And the next thing you know-

2769
01:52:26.336 --> 01:52:27.169
There was a cache to it,

2770
01:52:27.169 --> 01:52:29.815
even though mainstream people were hating it,

2771
01:52:29.815 --> 01:52:32.335
there was a core group of people who were the cool people

2772
01:52:32.335 --> 01:52:33.174
who are into it.

2773
01:52:33.174 --> 01:52:36.146
And the real inflection point was when...

2774
01:52:36.146 --> 01:52:38.347
And I think Kale says it in the documentary.

2775
01:52:38.347 --> 01:52:42.404
At some point during this tonal experiment,

2776
01:52:42.404 --> 01:52:46.154
somebody put an electric guitar pickup on it.

2777
01:52:47.205 --> 01:52:48.575
And then everything changed.

2778
01:52:48.575 --> 01:52:51.556
And then Lou Reed wasn't even part of it at the time,

2779
01:52:51.556 --> 01:52:53.013
there was this guy called Lou Reed

2780
01:52:53.013 --> 01:52:57.263
and he had a satchel full of songs and he enters it

2781
01:52:58.373 --> 01:53:02.037
after this tone experience is already kind of well underway.

2782
01:53:02.037 --> 01:53:02.870
And it comes to this-

2783
01:53:02.870 --> 01:53:03.703
Perfect.

2784
01:53:03.703 --> 01:53:06.855
Yeah, perfect storm that creates this whole new thing.

2785
01:53:06.855 --> 01:53:07.688
And then they add...

2786
01:53:07.688 --> 01:53:10.354
Then Andy Warhol becomes the manager

2787
01:53:10.354 --> 01:53:11.815
and then brings in Nico.

2788
01:53:11.815 --> 01:53:13.145
Which I didn't know anything about.

2789
01:53:13.145 --> 01:53:15.136
And insists, and fois Nico upon him.

2790
01:53:15.136 --> 01:53:17.886
And Nico's this glamazon gorgeous

2791
01:53:19.631 --> 01:53:23.947
Scandinavian who has this androgynous voice.

2792
01:53:23.947 --> 01:53:26.667
So it hits on so many Warhol levels,

2793
01:53:26.667 --> 01:53:31.667
the model type look with the androgyny and with this drone.

2794
01:53:31.848 --> 01:53:34.617
And obviously Lou is in that...

2795
01:53:34.617 --> 01:53:36.506
Lou came up when he was a teenager,

2796
01:53:36.506 --> 01:53:40.607
he was playing the gay clubs in New York City.

2797
01:53:40.607 --> 01:53:43.533
And like you said, they hated the hippies.

2798
01:53:43.533 --> 01:53:46.700
And so it's this very interesting mix.

2799
01:53:47.906 --> 01:53:51.856
And like I said to me, the two of these things together

2800
01:53:51.856 --> 01:53:56.256
kind of had me contemplating the nature of creativity,

2801
01:53:56.256 --> 01:53:57.786
which is not something I do very often.

2802
01:53:57.786 --> 01:53:59.953
But sometimes it comes up.

2803
01:54:00.842 --> 01:54:01.675
What is art?

2804
01:54:01.675 --> 01:54:02.508
What is music?

2805
01:54:02.508 --> 01:54:03.731
What is literary form?

2806
01:54:03.731 --> 01:54:06.046
What does any sort of scene or culture?

2807
01:54:06.046 --> 01:54:08.385
Isn't it just a collective dreaming

2808
01:54:08.385 --> 01:54:11.912
of what it means to be alive, to be human.

2809
01:54:11.912 --> 01:54:14.205
In all its ugliness and its beauty.

2810
01:54:14.205 --> 01:54:16.652
And now you have to...

2811
01:54:16.652 --> 01:54:18.355
This stuff has to hit for money to be made.

2812
01:54:18.355 --> 01:54:20.645
So he needed that dream together.

2813
01:54:20.645 --> 01:54:22.684
But that's not the primary driver and it's not

2814
01:54:22.684 --> 01:54:27.193
what brought John and Paul and George and Ringo together.

2815
01:54:27.193 --> 01:54:31.301
It's not what brings these people together.

2816
01:54:31.301 --> 01:54:34.535
It's not the underlying motivator of any scene.

2817
01:54:34.535 --> 01:54:36.035
Or any collective.

2818
01:54:38.005 --> 01:54:40.940
And to be a part of a scene, how cool is that?

2819
01:54:40.940 --> 01:54:42.823
That was always the dream coming up.

2820
01:54:42.823 --> 01:54:45.795
When I wanted to be a writer with my buddy Kelton Reed,

2821
01:54:45.795 --> 01:54:49.204
when we joined forces and we thought we loved the beats

2822
01:54:49.204 --> 01:54:51.212
and we love this era too.

2823
01:54:51.212 --> 01:54:52.994
And love reading Just Kids,

2824
01:54:52.994 --> 01:54:56.019
Patti Smith, being a part of that scene.

2825
01:54:56.019 --> 01:54:58.919
And you know, you are actually part of a scene.

2826
01:54:58.919 --> 01:55:00.910
You're like this early podcast.

2827
01:55:00.910 --> 01:55:03.470
I mean, this is its own type of scene.

2828
01:55:03.470 --> 01:55:04.598
It's more...

2829
01:55:04.598 --> 01:55:07.391
It's a little bit more removed because everyone's dislocated

2830
01:55:07.391 --> 01:55:08.268
in different places,

2831
01:55:08.268 --> 01:55:11.858
but you're very much on the forefront of this podcast scene.

2832
01:55:11.858 --> 01:55:13.678
And you're connected to some of these people

2833
01:55:13.678 --> 01:55:16.351
who are early innovators in the space.

2834
01:55:16.351 --> 01:55:20.160
Which is, I think another interesting art art form.

2835
01:55:20.160 --> 01:55:21.910
That's just starting to flourish.

2836
01:55:21.910 --> 01:55:24.743
And I was talking to not long ago,

2837
01:55:25.671 --> 01:55:29.140
the head of PR for audible about something else.

2838
01:55:29.140 --> 01:55:31.320
And she mentions neuroscience studies

2839
01:55:31.320 --> 01:55:36.042
about how part of the reason people love podcasts

2840
01:55:36.042 --> 01:55:38.767
and love audio books is when you listen,

2841
01:55:38.767 --> 01:55:40.000
versus when you read.

2842
01:55:40.000 --> 01:55:42.016
It hits a different part of your brain.

2843
01:55:42.016 --> 01:55:44.933
And it gets into the intimacy part.

2844
01:55:45.780 --> 01:55:47.809
Where that kind of lights up.

2845
01:55:47.809 --> 01:55:52.392
And so in it's way interacting as a different art form.

2846
01:55:53.602 --> 01:55:55.679
And the way people used to feel about music,

2847
01:55:55.679 --> 01:55:57.802
the way I felt about music or my favorite bands

2848
01:55:57.802 --> 01:55:59.018
and the merch we wore.

2849
01:55:59.018 --> 01:56:02.138
Is now shifting into the podcast and YouTube space

2850
01:56:02.138 --> 01:56:05.481
and people are getting merch for their favorite creators

2851
01:56:05.481 --> 01:56:06.868
in a whole different realm.

2852
01:56:06.868 --> 01:56:08.937
And this is the kind of stuff I was thinking about

2853
01:56:08.937 --> 01:56:09.787
while I was watching-

2854
01:56:09.787 --> 01:56:11.749
It's funny to...

2855
01:56:11.749 --> 01:56:13.389
I mean, yeah, I will take it as a compliment.

2856
01:56:13.389 --> 01:56:14.222
That's cool.

2857
01:56:14.222 --> 01:56:16.305
But my brain is thinking,

2858
01:56:17.223 --> 01:56:19.467
if this is a scene, I would trade this scene

2859
01:56:19.467 --> 01:56:22.217
to hang out with like Spalding Gray and David Byrne

2860
01:56:22.217 --> 01:56:24.674
and Lou Reed and Andy Warhol.

2861
01:56:24.674 --> 01:56:26.125
Or whatever was going on down there.

2862
01:56:26.125 --> 01:56:30.636
That just seems way more exciting and interesting.

2863
01:56:30.636 --> 01:56:32.505
And it's less sober, I think.

2864
01:56:32.505 --> 01:56:35.039
No, very much not a sober scene for sure.

2865
01:56:35.039 --> 01:56:37.703
Perilous probably would've killed me.

2866
01:56:37.703 --> 01:56:41.453
God spared me from being reared in that time and place.

2867
01:56:41.453 --> 01:56:42.724
You know what I'm saying right?

2868
01:56:42.724 --> 01:56:44.054
Yeah, I understand.

2869
01:56:44.054 --> 01:56:45.807
I mean, it's cool.

2870
01:56:45.807 --> 01:56:47.694
I do think...

2871
01:56:47.694 --> 01:56:50.715
And it's interesting because my sons are musicians

2872
01:56:50.715 --> 01:56:54.802
and I'm always thinking about the power of community.

2873
01:56:54.802 --> 01:56:56.294
Like what those scenes are.

2874
01:56:56.294 --> 01:56:58.282
You think of Grunge and you think of Seattle.

2875
01:56:58.282 --> 01:57:01.585
It's like what's going on in Nashville and country music.

2876
01:57:01.585 --> 01:57:02.944
And so I'm often like...

2877
01:57:02.944 --> 01:57:07.106
Or even LA at a period of time when like Beck was coming up.

2878
01:57:07.106 --> 01:57:10.464
There was kind of a scene at Spaceland and Silverlake

2879
01:57:10.464 --> 01:57:12.484
and all of that, but that doesn't really exist anymore.

2880
01:57:12.484 --> 01:57:13.704
Or Guns 'n Roses.

2881
01:57:13.704 --> 01:57:14.537
Sure.

2882
01:57:16.215 --> 01:57:20.132
The whole sunset strip metal era kind of thing.

2883
01:57:21.766 --> 01:57:23.107
So I always talk to them.

2884
01:57:23.107 --> 01:57:25.924
I'm like, "Tell me what is the scene like in LA?"

2885
01:57:25.924 --> 01:57:28.244
They're like, it's not really a scene.

2886
01:57:28.244 --> 01:57:29.080
There's a little bit of a scene.

2887
01:57:29.080 --> 01:57:30.509
I though that (indistinct) Bridgers has a scene.

2888
01:57:30.509 --> 01:57:31.869
Yeah, there's a little bit of that.

2889
01:57:31.869 --> 01:57:34.531
And they're kind of on the outer periphery

2890
01:57:34.531 --> 01:57:35.364
of that, I suppose.

2891
01:57:35.364 --> 01:57:39.781
But not like that scene where you feel like,

2892
01:57:39.781 --> 01:57:43.190
"Oh this is the place where this stuff like...

2893
01:57:43.190 --> 01:57:46.838
There's that palpable sense of something's happening here.

2894
01:57:46.838 --> 01:57:49.119
Because media is so siloed now.

2895
01:57:49.119 --> 01:57:50.771
And there's so much more of it.

2896
01:57:50.771 --> 01:57:55.559
So the saturation of so much content everywhere.

2897
01:57:55.559 --> 01:57:58.258
You can't possibly all be tuned into the same channel.

2898
01:57:58.258 --> 01:57:59.907
But back when the Beatles were coming up.

2899
01:57:59.907 --> 01:58:02.657
There weren't that many channels.

2900
01:58:03.607 --> 01:58:06.351
But if 56 Ludlow Street didn't exist,

2901
01:58:06.351 --> 01:58:09.149
does the Velvet Underground ever come to be?

2902
01:58:09.149 --> 01:58:10.879
No, but like Elizabeth Gilbert would say,

2903
01:58:10.879 --> 01:58:12.116
"Something else would."

2904
01:58:12.116 --> 01:58:14.551
The world didn't need Velvet Underground.

2905
01:58:14.551 --> 01:58:16.220
It's nice that it existed.

2906
01:58:16.220 --> 01:58:17.570
Something else would have been there.

2907
01:58:17.570 --> 01:58:18.957
And it'd been just as cool.

2908
01:58:18.957 --> 01:58:21.301
It just would have been different.

2909
01:58:21.301 --> 01:58:26.050
But now it's like there are so many more scenes.

2910
01:58:26.050 --> 01:58:28.671
And so then you have these kind of micro-climates

2911
01:58:28.671 --> 01:58:30.491
within that world.

2912
01:58:30.491 --> 01:58:31.440
In the context of the world.

2913
01:58:31.440 --> 01:58:35.319
Because everything's refracted and diffused.

2914
01:58:35.319 --> 01:58:37.152
There is no monoculture.

2915
01:58:37.152 --> 01:58:40.081
But these movements are always a contrast

2916
01:58:40.081 --> 01:58:42.533
to whatever the monoculture is.

2917
01:58:42.533 --> 01:58:45.400
No matter where culture exists,

2918
01:58:45.400 --> 01:58:49.363
the artists pendulum is swinging in the opposite direction.

2919
01:58:49.363 --> 01:58:51.621
And there is something to be said for proximity.

2920
01:58:51.621 --> 01:58:54.190
Now we have digital proximity that kind of resolves

2921
01:58:54.190 --> 01:58:55.219
a lot of that.

2922
01:58:55.219 --> 01:58:59.432
But, the idea that there are a bunch of artists

2923
01:58:59.432 --> 01:59:01.812
who are basically squatting in an apartment building

2924
01:59:01.812 --> 01:59:04.853
with nothing better to do than to collaborate all day long.

2925
01:59:04.853 --> 01:59:06.509
There is something unique about that.

2926
01:59:06.509 --> 01:59:07.342
Yeah right.

2927
01:59:07.342 --> 01:59:10.481
And the fact that cheap rent is a key to

2928
01:59:10.481 --> 01:59:12.841
kind of getting artists together is obviously well known.

2929
01:59:12.841 --> 01:59:14.260
That's what happened with Williamsburg

2930
01:59:14.260 --> 01:59:16.241
when Williams became a thing at the early 2000s

2931
01:59:16.241 --> 01:59:19.199
and plenty of bands came out of that era.

2932
01:59:19.199 --> 01:59:20.541
Seattle in the 90s.

2933
01:59:20.541 --> 01:59:22.341
Plenty of bands came out of that era.

2934
01:59:22.341 --> 01:59:26.678
You can't take real estate prices out of that realm there.

2935
01:59:26.678 --> 01:59:30.718
And so are rents, not real estate prices, but rent rates.

2936
01:59:30.718 --> 01:59:34.401
But it is interesting to think about.

2937
01:59:34.401 --> 01:59:36.098
And plus now it's more global, right?

2938
01:59:36.098 --> 01:59:40.181
We have access to Korean bands and African music.

2939
01:59:41.050 --> 01:59:43.578
And if you like drone music that you're hearing

2940
01:59:43.578 --> 01:59:45.259
in the Velvet Underground.

2941
01:59:45.259 --> 01:59:48.089
Check out the desert Tura guys that are coming

2942
01:59:48.089 --> 01:59:52.451
in Molly, and Algeria and listen to those guys.

2943
01:59:52.451 --> 01:59:55.861
Cause that's all blues meets drone.

2944
01:59:55.861 --> 01:59:58.947
Sure, and discovery is at your fingertips now.

2945
01:59:58.947 --> 02:00:01.818
Whereas it used to be difficult to find new stuff.

2946
02:00:01.818 --> 02:00:03.770
If you didn't have the cool older brother,

2947
02:00:03.770 --> 02:00:05.010
the guy to take you...

2948
02:00:05.010 --> 02:00:06.397
Or the guy at the record shop

2949
02:00:06.397 --> 02:00:08.331
who could tell you what was happening.

2950
02:00:08.331 --> 02:00:10.778
So it's right there for you.

2951
02:00:10.778 --> 02:00:12.795
So it's just a different era.

2952
02:00:12.795 --> 02:00:14.787
A couple of final thoughts on this documentary though.

2953
02:00:14.787 --> 02:00:16.620
I thought it was cool.

2954
02:00:17.749 --> 02:00:19.859
I didn't really realize that...

2955
02:00:19.859 --> 02:00:21.842
I mean you talk about Andy Warhol

2956
02:00:21.842 --> 02:00:22.910
kind of being in charge, right?

2957
02:00:22.910 --> 02:00:24.962
That had its pluses and minuses.

2958
02:00:24.962 --> 02:00:27.621
Without Warhol, does it ever get out of the gate?

2959
02:00:27.621 --> 02:00:30.524
And yet after a period of time,

2960
02:00:30.524 --> 02:00:33.803
they become stunted as a result of that relationship

2961
02:00:33.803 --> 02:00:36.503
and resentment around Nico and all of that kind of occurs.

2962
02:00:36.503 --> 02:00:40.403
That leads to ultimately the dismantling of the band

2963
02:00:40.403 --> 02:00:43.195
that never really saw the level of success

2964
02:00:43.195 --> 02:00:44.532
that I thought that it had.

2965
02:00:44.532 --> 02:00:46.369
It was interesting to hear,

2966
02:00:46.369 --> 02:00:49.243
they never really penetrated culture

2967
02:00:49.243 --> 02:00:51.005
in a way that I thought that they had that.

2968
02:00:51.005 --> 02:00:52.454
That comes much later.

2969
02:00:52.454 --> 02:00:54.414
And you know obviously Lou goes on

2970
02:00:54.414 --> 02:00:57.815
to this successful career outside of the Velvet Underground.

2971
02:00:57.815 --> 02:01:02.073
But what I thought was amazing was Sterling Morrison

2972
02:01:02.073 --> 02:01:04.886
goes and gets his PhD in English

2973
02:01:04.886 --> 02:01:06.256
and is a professor for a while.

2974
02:01:06.256 --> 02:01:08.473
And then he becomes a tugboat captain.

2975
02:01:08.473 --> 02:01:12.635
And then Mo the drummer is like an IT professional.

2976
02:01:12.635 --> 02:01:14.295
Can you imagine you're at your company?

2977
02:01:14.295 --> 02:01:17.494
And you have to go deal with the head of IT or whatever

2978
02:01:17.494 --> 02:01:21.582
and you realize she was the drummer in Velvet Underground.

2979
02:01:21.582 --> 02:01:23.041
But only the cool ones realized that.

2980
02:01:23.041 --> 02:01:24.977
Cause I bet a lot of people went in and out of that office

2981
02:01:24.977 --> 02:01:26.817
without even having any idea of who Mo Tucker was.

2982
02:01:26.817 --> 02:01:29.446
That's so amazing, that these lives

2983
02:01:29.446 --> 02:01:32.255
having full careers and lives

2984
02:01:32.255 --> 02:01:35.405
in the aftermath of being part of something

2985
02:01:35.405 --> 02:01:37.988
that was so culturally seismic.

2986
02:01:38.847 --> 02:01:41.862
Herman Melville became a customs officer after Moby Dick.

2987
02:01:41.862 --> 02:01:42.695
He did?

2988
02:01:42.695 --> 02:01:43.528
Yeah.

2989
02:01:43.528 --> 02:01:45.736
I didn't know that.

2990
02:01:45.736 --> 02:01:48.445
All right, let's do some listener questions.

2991
02:01:48.445 --> 02:01:49.885
Let's do it.

2992
02:01:49.885 --> 02:01:53.222
All right let's go to Al from DC.

2993
02:01:53.222 --> 02:01:54.163
Hello, Rich and Adam.

2994
02:01:54.163 --> 02:01:57.385
This is Al in DC, I'm calling with a question about

2995
02:01:57.385 --> 02:01:59.295
ramping up my endurance training.

2996
02:01:59.295 --> 02:02:01.423
I've been doing time restricted eating

2997
02:02:01.423 --> 02:02:03.666
and feeling great doing about an 18.6,

2998
02:02:03.666 --> 02:02:05.483
eating a lunch at about 02:00 o'clock

2999
02:02:05.483 --> 02:02:07.963
and stopping my caloric intake at about 08:00 o'clock.

3000
02:02:07.963 --> 02:02:10.995
But recently I've been ramping up on my running miles

3001
02:02:10.995 --> 02:02:13.613
training for 50K in the spring.

3002
02:02:13.613 --> 02:02:15.413
And I'm feeling like morning runs

3003
02:02:15.413 --> 02:02:17.914
doing 12 or 15 mile training runs

3004
02:02:17.914 --> 02:02:19.946
and going another four hours fasted.

3005
02:02:19.946 --> 02:02:21.574
After that run may not be optimal.

3006
02:02:21.574 --> 02:02:23.194
My gains and recovery.

3007
02:02:23.194 --> 02:02:25.205
I'm just curious if you have any insights

3008
02:02:25.205 --> 02:02:27.315
on how I should be adjusting my caloric intake

3009
02:02:27.315 --> 02:02:31.855
to optimize my gains during this training period.

3010
02:02:31.855 --> 02:02:34.415
Thank you for the question Al.

3011
02:02:34.415 --> 02:02:39.165
DC is a great running city, great place to train for 50K.

3012
02:02:40.714 --> 02:02:44.414
I think your instinct and your intuition is correct.

3013
02:02:44.414 --> 02:02:47.302
I appreciate the interest in self-experimentation

3014
02:02:47.302 --> 02:02:50.471
with restricted eating windows.

3015
02:02:50.471 --> 02:02:51.951
And I think it's fascinating,

3016
02:02:51.951 --> 02:02:56.241
the kind of science that's coming out around this

3017
02:02:56.241 --> 02:02:59.692
and the experimentation that so many athletes are doing.

3018
02:02:59.692 --> 02:03:02.758
My take on this with the caveat

3019
02:03:02.758 --> 02:03:06.441
that I think there's a lot of science that needs to be done

3020
02:03:06.441 --> 02:03:08.333
before we can get total clarity on this.

3021
02:03:08.333 --> 02:03:11.951
But as I see it now, my perspective is that

3022
02:03:11.951 --> 02:03:15.380
occasional fasted state workouts are okay.

3023
02:03:15.380 --> 02:03:19.363
And the science does support that there may be

3024
02:03:19.363 --> 02:03:21.580
a mild adaptation to that stress

3025
02:03:21.580 --> 02:03:25.191
that enhances aerobic state fat burning.

3026
02:03:25.191 --> 02:03:27.014
And I've experienced that myself

3027
02:03:27.014 --> 02:03:29.042
and I enjoy playing around with that.

3028
02:03:29.042 --> 02:03:32.139
But I think the emphasis has to be on occasional.

3029
02:03:32.139 --> 02:03:34.627
Because if you're training for a performance goal,

3030
02:03:34.627 --> 02:03:36.940
which you are doing right now,

3031
02:03:36.940 --> 02:03:41.940
the key I don't have to tell you to success is consistency.

3032
02:03:42.260 --> 02:03:46.231
Consistent training with an emphasis on

3033
02:03:46.231 --> 02:03:49.461
not only killing all your workouts,

3034
02:03:49.461 --> 02:03:51.470
but perhaps even more importantly,

3035
02:03:51.470 --> 02:03:54.520
optimizing your recovery between sessions.

3036
02:03:54.520 --> 02:03:56.573
And I think your current approach

3037
02:03:56.573 --> 02:04:01.406
of a persistent fasted state will not bode well with this.

3038
02:04:02.601 --> 02:04:06.993
You simply cannot perform day in and day out

3039
02:04:06.993 --> 02:04:08.520
with this fasting regimen,

3040
02:04:08.520 --> 02:04:12.549
without something going sideways at some point.

3041
02:04:12.549 --> 02:04:15.318
Or at a minimum failing to timely nourish yourself

3042
02:04:15.318 --> 02:04:17.799
is only gonna deplete you,

3043
02:04:17.799 --> 02:04:19.671
which you might be able to get through your workout

3044
02:04:19.671 --> 02:04:20.840
for the day and the next day.

3045
02:04:20.840 --> 02:04:24.109
But at some point it catches up to you

3046
02:04:24.109 --> 02:04:26.291
a couple of days later often.

3047
02:04:26.291 --> 02:04:28.465
And this is gonna impede your recovery.

3048
02:04:28.465 --> 02:04:31.098
It's gonna impair, potentially even depress

3049
02:04:31.098 --> 02:04:32.491
your immune system.

3050
02:04:32.491 --> 02:04:35.505
It may even lead to illness,

3051
02:04:35.505 --> 02:04:38.978
depriving your body of needed nutrients.

3052
02:04:38.978 --> 02:04:40.181
Which is only gonna undermine recovery

3053
02:04:40.181 --> 02:04:43.519
and thus the gains that you're seeking to achieve.

3054
02:04:43.519 --> 02:04:46.799
I don't think that I could be wrong,

3055
02:04:46.799 --> 02:04:49.491
but I don't think there's any elite athletes out there

3056
02:04:49.491 --> 02:04:53.113
who are trying to train in this manner

3057
02:04:53.113 --> 02:04:54.942
or who would entertain doing this.

3058
02:04:54.942 --> 02:04:57.239
You've gotta feed yourself.

3059
02:04:57.239 --> 02:04:58.621
You've gotta nourish yourself.

3060
02:04:58.621 --> 02:04:59.770
And as your training ramps up

3061
02:04:59.770 --> 02:05:02.353
and it becomes more and more...

3062
02:05:04.294 --> 02:05:05.631
Burdensome is the wrong word.

3063
02:05:05.631 --> 02:05:07.751
More and more onerous.

3064
02:05:07.751 --> 02:05:09.607
You're taxing your system in a way

3065
02:05:09.607 --> 02:05:11.580
that perhaps you haven't before,

3066
02:05:11.580 --> 02:05:13.137
if you wanna be able to bounce back

3067
02:05:13.137 --> 02:05:14.487
and do it the following day,

3068
02:05:14.487 --> 02:05:17.100
you're gonna have to feed those muscles and your body.

3069
02:05:17.100 --> 02:05:19.896
So again, fasted state, okay.

3070
02:05:19.896 --> 02:05:21.881
From time to time, occasionally.

3071
02:05:21.881 --> 02:05:23.390
I think it's good to stress the body

3072
02:05:23.390 --> 02:05:25.890
and establish physiologically,

3073
02:05:27.016 --> 02:05:29.707
and also psychologically that you're capable of going out

3074
02:05:29.707 --> 02:05:33.717
and doing a long run and not eating right away afterwards.

3075
02:05:33.717 --> 02:05:35.729
I think there's something to be said for that.

3076
02:05:35.729 --> 02:05:37.812
But not on a daily basis.

3077
02:05:38.919 --> 02:05:41.159
If you do wanna continue to play around with this,

3078
02:05:41.159 --> 02:05:42.689
I would suggest restricting

3079
02:05:42.689 --> 02:05:47.066
those fasted training days to maybe one at the most,

3080
02:05:47.066 --> 02:05:48.241
two days a week.

3081
02:05:48.241 --> 02:05:52.241
And to do it around your purely aerobic workouts

3082
02:05:53.765 --> 02:05:56.328
and to avoid it when you're doing tempo

3083
02:05:56.328 --> 02:05:58.609
or more anaerobic workouts.

3084
02:05:58.609 --> 02:06:01.068
So that's my two cents on that.

3085
02:06:01.068 --> 02:06:05.630
Again, I think what's going on here is interesting.

3086
02:06:05.630 --> 02:06:09.000
And like I said earlier, more science is needed

3087
02:06:09.000 --> 02:06:11.287
to figure this out.

3088
02:06:11.287 --> 02:06:12.620
Oh, "science".

3089
02:06:14.631 --> 02:06:15.645
You and your science.

3090
02:06:15.645 --> 02:06:17.392
You and your science.

3091
02:06:17.392 --> 02:06:19.781
I would fully agree with everything you just said.

3092
02:06:19.781 --> 02:06:20.614
Thank you, Adam.

3093
02:06:20.614 --> 02:06:25.112
I'm experimenting with a avocado toast diet right now.

3094
02:06:25.112 --> 02:06:26.825
Pre-workout or post.

3095
02:06:26.825 --> 02:06:28.035
Pre, during, post.

3096
02:06:28.035 --> 02:06:29.173
How long after your workout?

3097
02:06:29.173 --> 02:06:30.692
And there's zero fasting involved.

3098
02:06:30.692 --> 02:06:34.603
every hour it's more avocado toast for me.

3099
02:06:34.603 --> 02:06:35.436
When you're not eating,

3100
02:06:35.436 --> 02:06:37.755
I guess technically you're fasting.

3101
02:06:37.755 --> 02:06:41.052
You have like a 20 minute fasting window.

3102
02:06:41.052 --> 02:06:41.885
My biggest-

3103
02:06:41.885 --> 02:06:43.092
Your fastest state.

3104
02:06:43.092 --> 02:06:46.474
My biggest fasting window is on Roll On days.

3105
02:06:46.474 --> 02:06:48.102
We've gone a couple hours without you eating.

3106
02:06:48.102 --> 02:06:49.624
Are you okay?

3107
02:06:49.624 --> 02:06:52.924
Get me some avocado toast stat.

3108
02:06:52.924 --> 02:06:57.324
You're in your fat burning zone right now.

3109
02:06:57.324 --> 02:06:58.942
Great question, all right, cool.

3110
02:06:58.942 --> 02:07:02.525
Let's go to Kendall from Boulder, Colorado.

3111
02:07:03.515 --> 02:07:06.374
Hi guys, this is a Kendall

3112
02:07:06.374 --> 02:07:08.414
from Boulder, Colorado.

3113
02:07:08.414 --> 02:07:13.125
And my question and or point of discussion, I guess,

3114
02:07:13.125 --> 02:07:15.966
is the idea brought up in the conversation

3115
02:07:15.966 --> 02:07:17.046
with Reign and Riz

3116
02:07:17.046 --> 02:07:19.193
around the idea of the pursuit of happiness,

3117
02:07:19.193 --> 02:07:21.153
which is obviously an American thing.

3118
02:07:21.153 --> 02:07:25.646
And I think it's something I see show up on the pod a bit.

3119
02:07:25.646 --> 02:07:29.646
But I guess I ask, are we dismissing that aspect

3120
02:07:31.053 --> 02:07:34.525
of Life, Liberty and the pursuit of happiness?

3121
02:07:34.525 --> 02:07:37.858
Because we think that the idea is to achieve happiness.

3122
02:07:37.858 --> 02:07:40.356
Whereas it possibly could be that the forefathers

3123
02:07:40.356 --> 02:07:42.664
brought the idea of it being a pursuit

3124
02:07:42.664 --> 02:07:45.695
without the end ever being achieved.

3125
02:07:45.695 --> 02:07:46.846
To know that that was brought up

3126
02:07:46.846 --> 02:07:48.686
in a couple of different philosophers.

3127
02:07:48.686 --> 02:07:50.437
But I'm kind of curious what you guys think.

3128
02:07:50.437 --> 02:07:53.226
Do you think that the pursuit of happiness has an end,

3129
02:07:53.226 --> 02:07:56.604
or do you think that it was always meant to be a pursuit

3130
02:07:56.604 --> 02:07:58.894
and therefore the process along the way

3131
02:07:58.894 --> 02:08:00.816
is what creates the happiness.

3132
02:08:00.816 --> 02:08:04.821
Getting deep and philosophical with Kendall, from Boulder.

3133
02:08:04.821 --> 02:08:07.158
Deep and philosophical with the founding fathers?

3134
02:08:07.158 --> 02:08:07.991
I know.

3135
02:08:07.991 --> 02:08:11.430
Are we gonna be so bold as to try to read the minds

3136
02:08:11.430 --> 02:08:13.842
